swer-fields ["q-164469/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "140679a6-6a14-5f92-9a92-68917f197863"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-164469"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-164464/selection/0e92790933453368de79886a061edca87488b54fc5800aa6a40d5a25e4cfc6e2" :answer/id #uuid "f84d8e32-ed84-5c17-93bf-18460e56427b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164464/selection/d265c637482483a31cd8bd2c91e9421ca9fd540e68e230391e44c3a2e6bfdd59" :answer/id #uuid "fd9908df-fefe-5ee2-99e9-9024a6d6fed1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164464/selection/67d6eaac92f41a0979724cb216508636c37602fd2078e2feb1598a7959b1a9a7" :answer/id #uuid "07a98aea-06a5-5bd7-8f26-3a07ec62bc48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164464/selection/b6819a6540cd8053a629bae5855b89f4cc3e85d3a91d5c4ad4be477ef847624f" :answer/id #uuid "90d08ad7-3431-563b-83ad-add8bd4fe5c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164464/selection/1b2804e46a4091f34becb78ef8c5fed0fbcb4c61cd4fb4f59184c02acf5c5ca6" :answer/id #uuid "9ab1950f-cf5a-587e-a616-c8399f536b69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164464/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "44f7c18f-2e4c-5185-bd5a-c5ca02ccbfa8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-164464/selection/1b2804e46a4091f34becb78ef8c5fed0fbcb4c61cd4fb4f59184c02acf5c5ca6" :answer-field/choices ["q-164464/selection/0e92790933453368de79886a061edca87488b54fc5800aa6a40d5a25e4cfc6e2" "q-164464/selection/d265c637482483a31cd8bd2c91e9421ca9fd540e68e230391e44c3a2e6bfdd59" "q-164464/selection/67d6eaac92f41a0979724cb216508636c37602fd2078e2feb1598a7959b1a9a7" "q-164464/selection/b6819a6540cd8053a629bae5855b89f4cc3e85d3a91d5c4ad4be477ef847624f" "q-164464/selection/1b2804e46a4091f34becb78ef8c5fed0fbcb4c61cd4fb4f59184c02acf5c5ca6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-164464" :question/id #uuid "bdc1e9a7-73d7-57ba-931f-f608b20a72c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-164464" :question/problem "$$\nf(y,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $4x=16{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Solving our equation for $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n4x=16{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-164464/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "140679a6-6a14-5f92-9a92-68917f197863"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-164464"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-164538/selection/3241760d793c1deb77d5b8fed071cd166050543adeae163ee6445a08f221d6dc" :answer/id #uuid "328ebcf7-1142-5ac2-aa40-22a0a74a4e55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164538/selection/32e391f36a685a8ec3ffe98cf2a9bb1e88c341dc97ad138fe90842eccc4db81b" :answer/id #uuid "bffc4c57-ddca-5453-84c3-0fe04bb25902" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164538/selection/49a88440a8da8caadaaafc890bfd63ff18b29bb64cc755c31bbd28788b18345d" :answer/id #uuid "b77356bd-a7c4-5ebb-a001-12c52e15d793" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164538/selection/7f617aa0c8dc58f9a375438b7c8ea30924287e5fe23f5e4ae214340ac1c2ab61" :answer/id #uuid "020dd5c5-796c-5b7c-9c37-cb18499b2254" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164538/selection/5ee175926093542d7e147978bcba27ae91efae19c8e0c8029d48db1866347ced" :answer/id #uuid "1b5f67d7-e6af-5b58-a116-e7a1ada1d280" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164538/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e6917248-de85-5455-9b75-7282feb004f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-164538/selection/7f617aa0c8dc58f9a375438b7c8ea30924287e5fe23f5e4ae214340ac1c2ab61" :answer-field/choices ["q-164538/selection/3241760d793c1deb77d5b8fed071cd166050543adeae163ee6445a08f221d6dc" "q-164538/selection/32e391f36a685a8ec3ffe98cf2a9bb1e88c341dc97ad138fe90842eccc4db81b" "q-164538/selection/49a88440a8da8caadaaafc890bfd63ff18b29bb64cc755c31bbd28788b18345d" "q-164538/selection/7f617aa0c8dc58f9a375438b7c8ea30924287e5fe23f5e4ae214340ac1c2ab61" "q-164538/selection/5ee175926093542d7e147978bcba27ae91efae19c8e0c8029d48db1866347ced"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-164538" :question/id #uuid "c506193a-85c6-5094-962b-7c03d5841210" :question/math-academy-id "q-164538" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,{r}^{2},\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=5X\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=5X\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3Y,Z,5X⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$  \n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-164538/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ef56d-869d-5939-9a0a-84477cf5a074"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-164538"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-164541"] :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=2X,\\,z=Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=2X,\\,z=Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get \n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,2X,Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-164529/selection/cccd5b6491b8e21755c4616b9923504f2ecc132a9b4b5e0045dfd0bf07995d92" :answer/id #uuid "c7e8c89d-dfe8-501a-bf65-95c3c6baa8c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164529/selection/99be318b5a6726e3c85484fb705a89d5495f7b936a58424edee2ec3b7cc5f9ba" :answer/id #uuid "a6e22e8c-9c7a-550b-a6d5-cd39a1eb593a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164529/selection/fd538f94cd15d4adec9cf0ec2fa6ad67e3af45816557e8baeb0500f4f518c1e9" :answer/id #uuid "3648bd95-7162-5076-ac98-667f5353a545" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164529/selection/3241760d793c1deb77d5b8fed071cd166050543adeae163ee6445a08f221d6dc" :answer/id #uuid "018b8a08-b6bf-5dc7-8fe4-92c8ecc3ef70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164529/selection/7f617aa0c8dc58f9a375438b7c8ea30924287e5fe23f5e4ae214340ac1c2ab61" :answer/id #uuid "c52a6769-f25d-5aba-9ab2-99be1a5ff558" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164529/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "be23ae1d-c459-5973-9102-bf6e3310a126" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-164529/selection/cccd5b6491b8e21755c4616b9923504f2ecc132a9b4b5e0045dfd0bf07995d92" :answer-field/choices ["q-164529/selection/cccd5b6491b8e21755c4616b9923504f2ecc132a9b4b5e0045dfd0bf07995d92" "q-164529/selection/99be318b5a6726e3c85484fb705a89d5495f7b936a58424edee2ec3b7cc5f9ba" "q-164529/selection/fd538f94cd15d4adec9cf0ec2fa6ad67e3af45816557e8baeb0500f4f518c1e9" "q-164529/selection/3241760d793c1deb77d5b8fed071cd166050543adeae163ee6445a08f221d6dc" "q-164529/selection/7f617aa0c8dc58f9a375438b7c8ea30924287e5fe23f5e4ae214340ac1c2ab61"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-164529" :question/id #uuid "ae578e5d-9060-5ab0-8bf3-d4a37a35851c" :question/math-academy-id "q-164529" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,{r}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4X,\\sqrt{3}Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-164529/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ef56d-869d-5939-9a0a-84477cf5a074"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-164529"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-164539/selection/a8c128ec19f546285e48ac08753c7a9ff9e7ba03ab141aedb59d2c8fa1083a43" :answer/id #uuid "26502d6f-2334-5f6f-80d9-196639647c77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164539/selection/a776c264a990bf68bb2034eaeca84e0ebdd6ce793d1fc60e9c650d85d1d1add6" :answer/id #uuid "0626cb92-a43a-5bdc-baac-5d39f1b31a93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164539/selection/7f617aa0c8dc58f9a375438b7c8ea30924287e5fe23f5e4ae214340ac1c2ab61" :answer/id #uuid "a0a90247-2195-5150-97bd-3184aa465e82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164539/selection/ad786dd6a99b61983f2170388da49b231a900d7d8cdc651d22ebb6f2363b4e25" :answer/id #uuid "de0eb1b2-2aeb-5ded-b979-0cd267672d54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164539/selection/3241760d793c1deb77d5b8fed071cd166050543adeae163ee6445a08f221d6dc" :answer/id #uuid "ac4573f7-5442-5de8-bc37-b2d60d727eb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164539/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "03a12b12-d2a1-53b8-a55f-0b66b60709e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-164539/selection/ad786dd6a99b61983f2170388da49b231a900d7d8cdc651d22ebb6f2363b4e25" :answer-field/choices ["q-164539/selection/a8c128ec19f546285e48ac08753c7a9ff9e7ba03ab141aedb59d2c8fa1083a43" "q-164539/selection/a776c264a990bf68bb2034eaeca84e0ebdd6ce793d1fc60e9c650d85d1d1add6" "q-164539/selection/7f617aa0c8dc58f9a375438b7c8ea30924287e5fe23f5e4ae214340ac1c2ab61" "q-164539/selection/ad786dd6a99b61983f2170388da49b231a900d7d8cdc651d22ebb6f2363b4e25" "q-164539/selection/3241760d793c1deb77d5b8fed071cd166050543adeae163ee6445a08f221d6dc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-164539" :question/id #uuid "e29c164b-752c-5fa5-bd77-ca5f63d2912a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164539" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨{r}^{2},\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,3X,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{r}^{2},\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-164539/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ef56d-869d-5939-9a0a-84477cf5a074"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-164539"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-164536/selection/4396e9b5b378e73f37c569a5dc88d1d38b572730f898e9f6aaa324fb2f1150b0" :answer/id #uuid "1ddfbc20-fb88-5b94-a62d-b98f81820201" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2r" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164536/selection/6963cb0644028a5ac2383f25c9029d059ac1b2d59de0540a438d0395644d578f" :answer/id #uuid "1ebdb291-9059-58b5-a0b5-906a76e2a088" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164536/selection/ad786dd6a99b61983f2170388da49b231a900d7d8cdc651d22ebb6f2363b4e25" :answer/id #uuid "ff5bce58-6411-568b-829e-bd203d7a3ea3" :answer/type :answer.type/mat