 "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=-25.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=25$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨z,r⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-164570/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece6a5a-e9b2-5531-85f0-3774d0e3f966"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-164570"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-164581/selection/11095f1eb160eefcd18ed934cd18ebfa6380ea4a2e4f162a1c1b547a4875af87" :answer/id #uuid "2a01a4e0-2a7f-5b87-8449-9c7678ef63d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164581/selection/53c9cc53c9151a84ee0fa4eb42f3c6e72ec949096bdc0a8e9511a23936508243" :answer/id #uuid "6e05263a-f9d8-57ec-a811-1a4a37ea7ff1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164581/selection/2eb4d3f4e3d13d5e12330258b89ddabb2fc4c9c19a9ed2ae63449c219e2d44d7" :answer/id #uuid "89eb5ffc-273b-5a65-b9a5-ecba76f77b2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164581/selection/2d84281d901b8d83d5ea9be094e975d563b73610fe96a43406a037db2b9740b4" :answer/id #uuid "72d91634-e4a4-5e81-93ed-06b612f16f84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164581/selection/17480af3c85f2c9135fdfe4f9ff248cf3a56f2e755cad1fa852467580f33b148" :answer/id #uuid "219bd5d1-c90c-5cd1-b854-d34cdc4ed7eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164581/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a6da799e-54ff-56a2-a303-ef67bbef8c4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-164581/selection/2eb4d3f4e3d13d5e12330258b89ddabb2fc4c9c19a9ed2ae63449c219e2d44d7" :answer-field/choices ["q-164581/selection/11095f1eb160eefcd18ed934cd18ebfa6380ea4a2e4f162a1c1b547a4875af87" "q-164581/selection/53c9cc53c9151a84ee0fa4eb42f3c6e72ec949096bdc0a8e9511a23936508243" "q-164581/selection/2eb4d3f4e3d13d5e12330258b89ddabb2fc4c9c19a9ed2ae63449c219e2d44d7" "q-164581/selection/2d84281d901b8d83d5ea9be094e975d563b73610fe96a43406a037db2b9740b4" "q-164581/selection/17480af3c85f2c9135fdfe4f9ff248cf3a56f2e755cad1fa852467580f33b148"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-164581" :question/id #uuid "c7dbeb7e-e0a8-5864-a21a-43fc2742343e" :question/math-academy-id "q-164581" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-{z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨Z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {Z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨Z,r⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,2Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-164581/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece6a5a-e9b2-5531-85f0-3774d0e3f966"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-164581"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8891"] :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(y, z) =  \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(y, z)$ above parametrizes the hyperbolic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $-3x=-6y^2+3z^2.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Solving our equation for $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n-3x=-6y^2+3z^2 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx=2y^2-z^2.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} 2y^2-z^2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12561"] :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta),$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =  \\big\\langle \\text{_____} , \\: r^2, \\: 2 r \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\ny=4{x^2}+\\dfrac{z^2}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx =\\dfrac1{2}X, \\qquad\ny = Z, \\qquad\nz = 2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $\\mathbf{f}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx =\\dfrac1{2}X, \\qquad\ny = Z, \\qquad\nz = 2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get \n\n$$\nZ = X^2 + Y^2.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\nY = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nZ = z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}X,Z,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r \\in [0,\\infty)$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} \\dfrac1{2} r \\cos\\theta}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8886"] :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi),$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) =  \\big\\langle 3\\cosh{\\phi} \\cos\\theta, \\: 4\\sinh{\\phi}, \\: \\text{______} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{9}-\\dfrac{y^2}{16}+z^2=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx = 3X, \\qquad\ny = 4Z, \\qquad\nz = Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $\\mathbf{f}.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $r^2-Z^2 = k^2$ can be parametrized as $\\langle r, Z\\rangle = \\langle k\\cosh\\phi, k\\sinh \\phi\\rangle$* for $\\phi\\in \\mathbb R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx = 3X, \\qquad\ny = 4Z, \\qquad\nz = Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nX^2 + Y^2 - Z^2 = 1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\nY = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nZ = Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola $r^2 - Z^2 = 1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta$ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n\\langle r, Z \\rangle = \\langle \\cosh{\\phi}, \\: \\sinh{\\phi} \\rangle,\n\\qquad \\phi \\in \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,4Z,Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4Z,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} \\cosh{\\phi} \\sin\\theta }.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8890"] :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi),$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) =  \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 3\\sinh{\\phi} \\sin\\theta, \\: 3\\cosh{\\phi} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = -9.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $\\mathbf{f}.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $z^2-r^2 = k^2$ can be parametrized as $\\langle z,r\\rangle = \\langle k\\cosh\\phi, k\\sinh \\phi\\rangle$* for $\\phi\\in \\mathbb R.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\n\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \n\nz = z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola $z^2 - r^2 = 9$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/assets/4e7b508432f12b8dc98e37ae2786d220eb97bdc58fe5a29c5f7728cac46471f0.png)\n\nUsing the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n\\langle z, r \\rangle = \\langle 3\\cosh{\\phi}, \\: 3\\sinh{\\phi} \\rangle,\n\\qquad \\phi \\in \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}3\\sinh{\\phi} \\cos\\theta} .$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
